azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net.  Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

And a-waaay we go....

> [1]: Spinach golem: The rarest monster in nethack; a living specimen
> has never been seen. Its corpse is sometimes encountered, but only in
> tins. -azgor

What is it with the weird golem types?  All the golems currently in the
game match some material in the game, and are created when that
material is polymorphed in excess.  Spinach isn't a material, so
spinach golems are not a good idea.  (The same goes for cockatrice
golems, from the last YANI collection.)

> [2]: Each role should have a default name for his pets (as in Samurai
> => Hachi) -moussambani

Interesting.  For tourists, cats should be Greebo (or Maurice), dogs
should be Gaspode, and horses, Binky.  For archaeologists, dogs should
be named Indiana....

> [3]: Tigers should occasionally drop tigereye on death. This should
> glow brightly for several hundred turns, and be chargable. (Tiger,
> Tiger, burning bright) -SpeedEvil

I like Blake as much as the next guy, and Nethack, far more than most
other games, is great for those kinds of literary references, but this
is stretching it.

> [4]: When attacking a Jaguar with a pickaxe or mattock, the Jaguar
> should eat the weapon, and display the message "Oh no, that was a 2
> litre Jaguar". -SpeedEvil

I don't get it.

> [5]: all felines should - when the player has a light, be visible as 'f
> - an unknown feline' at double the normal distance, due to their
> reflective eyes. -SpeedEvil

Oddly interesting (although not too compelling), although it should be
noted that cats' eyes glow because they reflect light.  So, in cases
there there's no other light nearby, they should not be so visible from
a distance.

> [6]: The Gazebo. A golem. It engulfs, but does no damage. Vulnerable to
> fire. -SpeedEvil

Too random.

> [7]: Corpses of unique monsters could be worth much cash and points.
> -GreyKnight

Worth cash to who?  To much trouble to carry around (along with an ice
box), and there's no good reason for corpses to be worth extra points.

> [8]: If player is wearing a ring of sustain ability while polymorphing
> into a creature which has lower stat(s) than the previous form had, the
> ring has a chance of exploding or flying in a random direction. -Zanasj

Nah.

> [9]: Flesh golem corpses should be a bit like tins, before eating:
> "This smells like <random_monster> flesh. Eat it? [y/n]". Of course
> possibly giving appropriate intrinsics etc. -Duke-

For this to make sense, flesh golems themselves must work that way,
which would also mean that flesh golems would have to randomly have
certain extra, perhaps highly dangerous, properties.  Yes, I'm thinking
about cockatrices....

> [10]: Magic golem, made of various magical accoutrements (magical
> potions, scrolls, wands, spellbooks). Can breathe magic missiles and is
> resistant to magic. -GreyKnight

See above, about golems and materials.

> [11]: If a camera is destroyed with N charges or more remaining, a
> yellow light pops out of the remains. For bonus fun, produce a black
> light instead if the camera was cursed. -GreyKnight [N lights might be
> even more fun. -ed.]

Kind of interesting, but falls into the random whimsy category.  It
doesn't improve the game except as yet another exceedingly minor
special case in the code, and this one's not too logical either: yellow
lights are magical, while cameras are technological.

> [12]: new monster: albatross.  represented as white B.  arrows and
> crossbow bolts do double damage.  always leaves a corpse unless it came
> back via #turn undead.  taming one provides +3 luck while it's still
> your pet,  killing one is -3 luck.  Relatively rare in appearance,
> non-genocidable, non-figurinable.  Corpse can be worn as an amulet in
> the amulet slot; until it decays, it will act as a non-turning-to-dust
> AoLS.  While wearing the albatross corpse, your luck is fixed at -3.
> -lorimer

Another Nethack literary reference is often cool.  There could be some
interesting aspects to this, but this specific idea doesn't strike me
as "Nethacky" somehow.

> [13]: New monster: Brownie. A gnome like D&D character. Should be less
> powerful than a gnome and [inhabit] upper gnomish mines. -Gaulgath

What other abilities would it have?  Possibly interesting depending on
its abilities.

> [14]: When a boulder ... falls on an altar it should crush it. ie
> reading a scroll of earth on top of an altar. -Gaulgath

This seems logical, although if this is done, then pushing a boulder
onto an altar should also have a chance of destroying it.  High Altars
should be immune to such crushing.  Of course, destroying an altar
should anger any attached priest.  This might cause trouble with
temples in general.  (Does a temple room without an altar still count
as a temple?)

> [15]: Attempting to #name a specific object, especially when trying
> things like the Helm of Brilliance identification trick, should break
> illiterate conduct --- the text of the game specifically says "Your
> hand slips while engraving" and the information stays around even if
> you get flayed. -raxvulpine

Interesting, and logical.

> [16]: Entering Delphi as a woodchuck should produce a YAFM. -HappyFoppy
> [What message? -ed.]

I agree, without the message this isn't much of a YANI....

> [17]: Getting a towel wet should transform it into a wet towel, with a
> corresponding increase in weight and a devastating increase in damage.
> -Grunt

Wetting a towel is logical, but should not make it a particularly good
weapon.  I mean, it might sting, but it's not a blade for heaven's
sake.

> [18]: More gourmet tin messages. "You consume terrine of Green-elf in a
> delicate black pudding sauce." "You consume salt-and-cardamom-cured
> kraken gravlax." -cjeris

Nethack probably has enough of those already.  I mean, it already has
schezuan tins!

> [19]: Grant a bonus to effective Int+Cha for foocubus interaction if
> the player is wielding a bullwhip, carrying an iron chain, or both.

This assumes the 'cubus likes that kind of thing.

- John H.



